        "Jacques F. A. P. Miller",
        "Max D. Cooper"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The demonstration of distinct B- and T-cell lineages established a central organizing principle of adaptive immunity with enduring explanatory and clinical importance.",
        "attribution": "Miller and Cooper represent complementary decisive work defining thymus-dependent and bursa-equivalent lymphocyte systems.",
        "maturity": "The finding has been repeatedly confirmed and integrated across modern immunology for decades.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The historical contribution involved several experimental traditions, making the exact boundary of the awardable discovery necessarily selective."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B029",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Douglas R. Lowy",
        "Ian H. Frazer",
        "John T. Schiller"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "HPV virus-like-particle vaccines provide unusually clear prevention of infection and associated cancers, combining a major biological insight with global medical benefit.",
        "attribution": "Lowy, Frazer, and Schiller are strongly associated with the conceptual and experimental route to prophylactic VLP vaccination.",
        "maturity": "Long-term population evidence and broad implementation establish both efficacy and public-health significance.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "Translation to licensed vaccines also depended on substantial industrial and clinical-development contributions."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B020",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Francis S. Collins",
        "John R. Riordan",
        "Lap-Chee Tsui"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Identifying CFTR made cystic fibrosis a molecularly intelligible disease and enabled a long chain of diagnosis, mechanism, and therapy.",
        "attribution": "Collins, Riordan, and Tsui provide a coherent three-person attribution for the mapping and cloning achievement.",
        "maturity": "The gene assignment and its physiological significance are definitive, with its translational consequences now well established.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The award would need to distinguish gene discovery from the later functional-rescue work represented elsewhere on the list."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B042",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Arthur L. Horwich",
        "F. Ulrich Hartl"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Chaperonin-assisted folding transformed understanding of how cells attain and protect functional protein structure.",
        "attribution": "Horwich and Hartl are the principal complementary contributors to the genetic and biochemical demonstration of chaperonin-mediated folding.",
        "maturity": "The mechanism is deeply established and influential across cell biology, proteostasis, and disease research.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "Protein-folding research is broad, so a citation would require a sharply defined focus on chaperonins."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B012",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Ronald M. Evans"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The nuclear-receptor framework provided a powerful account of ligand-regulated transcription and connected endocrine signaling to gene control.",
        "attribution": "Evans is a leading and readily identifiable contributor, though the superfamily was elucidated by a wide international field.",
        "maturity": "The basic biology and pharmacological relevance have been confirmed over decades.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "A single-person award may underrepresent foundational contributions by other receptor discoverers."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B004",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Donald R. Senger",
        "Harold F. Dvorak",
        "Napoleone Ferrara"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "VEGF biology and anti-VEGF treatment linked a fundamental driver of angiogenesis to major benefits in ophthalmic and cancer medicine.",
        "attribution": "Senger, Dvorak, and Ferrara cover important discovery, biological characterization, and therapeutic-development contributions.",
        "maturity": "Pathological angiogenesis inhibition is a mature clinical paradigm with extensive validation.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The allocation of priority among early vascular-permeability-factor and VEGF work is historically complex."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B037",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Kazutoshi Mori",
        "Peter Walter"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The unfolded protein response revealed how cells sense and adapt to stress in the endoplasmic reticulum, a core mechanism of cellular homeostasis.",
        "attribution": "Mori and Walter made defining mechanistic contributions to major arms of the response.",
        "maturity": "The pathway is mechanistically mature and has broad relevance to physiology and disease.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "Its many branches and contributors make a concise attribution challenging."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B036",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Adrian R. Krainer",
        "C. Frank Bennett"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Antisense correction of SMN2 splicing established a compelling route from RNA mechanism to transformative treatment for spinal muscular atrophy.",
        "attribution": "Krainer and Bennett represent the key mechanistic and therapeutic-development sides of the advance.",
        "maturity": "Clinical benefit is robust and the approach has demonstrated a durable translational impact.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The contribution is recent relative to several foundational candidates and involved extensive collaborative clinical development."
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
